Subject: RegExp: the pristine check validates RegExp.prototype and RegExp by content; no counted reference to their shapes

What this does
  - quickjs.c js_regexp_snapshot kept a counted reference on the shapes of RegExp.prototype and of RegExp (ctx->re_proto_shape, re_ctor_shape) and the pristine check compared the objects' shapes with them. RegExp.prototype was built by adding properties one at a time, so its shape is not hashed, and a shape that is not hashed must have exactly one owner: the next property add (add_property asserts JS_REF_COUNT(p->shape) == 1) or in-place edit then failed, and, because an unhashed shape is edited where it stands, the pointer comparison also stayed equal across the edit.
  - The context now keeps, for each snapshot property, a counted atom and the slot, and the prototype's own prototype. js_regexp_proto_pristine checks that the slot is in range, still carries the same name, is still a getter or a data property as before, and still holds the same function; js_regexp_ctor_pristine does the same for the Symbol.species getter. The shapes themselves are not referenced.

Why we need it
  tests/differential/corpus/regexp-flags-derivation.js aborts (add_property assertion) when it deletes RegExp.prototype.dotAll and defines it again. Patch 0531 introduced the held shapes.

Failure modes
  The pristine check reads one shape-property entry more per snapshot property (about 13) than before; the row is unchanged within noise.

